        "The founder describes a turning point: the team asked what dropshippers actually cared about and what they were using most. They concluded that the product needed a professional-quality theme, strong product pages, useful theme sections, bundles, and upsells.\n\nThe lesson is to stop building around internal assumptions and rebuild around the customer's actual job.",
        "Map the customer's workflow\nFor a customer using an ecommerce store-building tool, the workflow might include:\n1. Create a store.\n2. Choose and customise a theme.\n3. Build product pages.\n4. Add useful page sections.\n5. Configure bundles.\n6. Set up upsells.\n7. Test the buying experience.\n8. Monitor performance.\n9. Improve the store.\n\nThis is a workflow map based on the product capabilities described in the transcript, not a claim that every customer follows exactly this sequence.",
        "Product strategy: solve the whole job, not an isolated feature\nA tool that generates a store once may be useful. But if customers also need a high-quality theme, product pages, bundles, and upsells, a more complete platform can become more valuable.\n\nThe product strategy becomes:\n- Identify the most important customer workflow.\n- Find where users currently switch between tools.\n- Determine which parts can be integrated.\n- Make the experience faster and more reliable.\n- Maintain quality across the whole workflow.",
        "Product quality review\nFor each core feature, evaluate:\n\n| Dimension | Review question |\n| --- | --- |\n| Usefulness | Does it help the customer achieve a meaningful result? |\n| Quality | Is the output good enough for real use? |\n| Speed | Does it reduce time or effort? |\n| Reliability | Does it work consistently? |\n| Integration | Does it fit naturally into the wider workflow? |\n| Clarity | Can users understand how to use it? |\n| Support | Can problems be diagnosed and resolved? |",
        "The “would I use it?” test\nThe founder says the team wanted to build something so good that he would use it himself. This is a useful quality test, but it is not sufficient on its own. Founders are not always representative of their customers.\n\nCombine personal use with:\n- Customer interviews.\n- Usability testing.\n- Behavioural data.\n- Retention analysis.\n- Support-ticket analysis.\n- Customer outcome measurement.\n\nPart 6 — Measure Customer Outcomes and Create Distribution"
